###  https://w3id.org/aief/AIAAIC_071
:AIAAIC_071 rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                     :Incident ;
            :demonstratesRisk :Discrimination ,
                              :PrivacyBreach ;
            :impactsRight :Art21_NonDiscrimination ,
                          :Art48_PresumptionOfInnocence ,
                          :Art8_DataProtection ;
            :aiCapability "Reasoning (Risk Scoring)" ;
            :incidentID "AIAAIC-071" ;
            :incidentTitle "SyRI Welfare Fraud Detection System Ruled Unlawful (Netherlands)" ;
            :incidentYear "2020" ;
            :noteText "The Hague District Court (NJCM c.s. v. The Netherlands, ECLI:NL:RBDHA:2020:1878) ruled the System Risk Indication (SyRI) algorithm, used to flag welfare recipients as fraud-risk suspects on unspecified profiling criteria, violated Article 8 ECHR, in part because opaque risk-flagging of citizens as suspects prior to any finding of wrongdoing was incompatible with a fair balance between fraud prevention and privacy/non-discrimination rights." .


###  https://w3id.org/aief/AIAAIC_072
:AIAAIC_072 rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                     :Incident ;
            :demonstratesRisk :Discrimination ;
            :impactsRight :Art21_NonDiscrimination ,
                          :Art48_PresumptionOfInnocence ,
                          :Art6_RightToLiberty ;
            :aiCapability "Reasoning (Predictive Policing)" ;
            :incidentID "AIAAIC-072" ;
            :incidentTitle "Predictive Policing Programs Discontinued After Bias Findings (US)" ;
            :incidentYear "2020" ;
            :noteText "Multiple US police departments (including LAPD's PredPol/PredCrime deployment) discontinued place-based and person-based predictive policing programs following independent audits and investigative reporting finding the underlying arrest data reproduced historical over-policing of minority neighbourhoods, treating past policing intensity as a proxy for future criminality prior to any individualised suspicion." .


###  https://w3id.org/aief/AIAAIC_073
:AIAAIC_073 rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                     :Incident ;
            :demonstratesRisk :Discrimination ;
            :impactsRight :Art13_FreedomOfArtsScience ,
                          :Art21_NonDiscrimination ;
            :aiCapability "Reasoning (NLP Classification)" ;
            :incidentID "AIAAIC-073" ;
            :incidentTitle "AI-Detection Tools Falsely Flag Non-Native English Academic Writing" ;
            :incidentYear "2023" ;
            :noteText "Liang et al. (2023, arXiv:2304.02819) found widely-deployed GPT-detection tools misclassified over half of TOEFL essays by non-native English writers as AI-generated, with universities using such tools for academic-integrity enforcement; students and researchers face restricted ability to pursue academic work under suspicion generated by an unaudited classifier, directly implicating the freedom to conduct academic research (Art 13)." .


